<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hormuzd Rassam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hormuzd Rassam was an Assyriologist and diplomat, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> to an Assyrian Christian family, who worked as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a>'s assistant and later excavated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. In 1853 he found the North Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> with much of its library and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-hunt-of-ashurbanipal">lion hunt reliefs</a>, and in 1879 he discovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam.jpg" alt="Hormuzd Rassam" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Henry Delamotte (England: 1820 - 1889) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hormuzd.Rassam.reclined.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rassam was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. His grandfather, Anton Rassam, was archdeacon of the Chaldean Catholic Church there, and his mother Theresa was a daughter of Isaak Halabee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Through his brother, the British Vice-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> in Mosul, he was hired in 1846, at the age of 20, as paymaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a>, who became a lifelong friend. Layard arranged for him to study for 18 months at Magdalen College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, before the second expedition of 1849 to 1851.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Layard left for politics, Rassam carried on the work at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 1852–54, finding among much else the tablets that George Smith later deciphered as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>. He then entered government service at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, rising to First Political Resident. In 1866 he was sent with a letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, who held British missionaries hostage. Received well at first, he was himself made a prisoner and held for two years, until Robert Napier's expedition of 1868 defeated the emperor. Newspapers portrayed him as ineffectual, but ministers of both parties defended him, and the Queen gave him £5,000.</p><p>From 1877 to 1882 he led four expeditions for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> under an agreement Layard, then ambassador at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, had made with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>. He found the Ashurnasirpal temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, two bronze strips of the Balawat Gates, a palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a> and, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> in 1881, the temple of the sun with some 50,000 tablets of its accounts. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877 to 1878 he also reported on the Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rassam settled in Brighton, married Anne Eliza Price and wrote on Assyrian exploration and the Christian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Sir Henry Rawlinson claimed credit for finding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s palace and called him a mere digger, and in 1893 Rassam sued E. A. Wallis Budge for slander and libel over charges of smuggling, winning the courts' support. He died in September 1910 and was buried in Hove Cemetery. A 21st-century account charges him with extensive, unrecorded digging that amounted to looting of many sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hormuzd Rassam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Library of Ashurbanipal, rediscovered at Nineveh by Austen Henry Layard and <b>Hormuzd Rassam</b> in the mid-nineteenth century, preserved the standard version of the Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, along with thousands of texts on Mesopotamian divination, medicine and religion that might…</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hormuzd Rassam</b> found the Cylinder in March 1879, during excavations at Babylon for the British Museum under an Ottoman firman.</blockquote><a href="/work/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>About 15,000 fragments of Assyrian cuneiform tablets were found in the Library of Ashurbanipal in Nineveh by Austen Henry Layard, his assistant <b>Hormuzd Rassam</b> and W. K. Loftus in the early 1850s; Rassam found the Standard Babylonian version there in 1853.</blockquote><a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>…the ruins of the palace and library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal (668–626 BCE) at Nineveh: Austen Henry Layard recovered fragments in 1849, and in the excavations of Layard, <b>Hormuzd Rassam</b> and George Smith at Kuyunjik between 1848 and 1876, Smith identified creation texts among some 20,000 fragments.</blockquote><a href="/songs/enuma-elis">Enūma Eliš</a></li><li><blockquote>About 15,000 fragments of cuneiform tablets were found in the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh by Austen Henry Layard, <b>Hormuzd Rassam</b> and W. K. Loftus in the early 1850s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Hove, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1880" aria-label="Mosul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hormuzd Rassam"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5897 4407h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5897 4407h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1880">What was here in 1880? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
